# SerdSafely

Private file delivery. nginx in front, a Python (FastAPI) backend behind it,
files in S3.

Delivery only: the app reads from the bucket and never writes to it. Files are
put there by some other route, and the task role carries no write permission.

nginx serves no HTML of its own: the front page and the custom 4xx/5xx pages
are all proxied from the backend, so one codebase decides what this service
looks like. The single exception is a static "temporarily unavailable" page,
used only when the backend is the thing that has failed and so cannot render
one itself.

## Layout

```
backend/app/
  main.py         app, exception handlers, start-up
  config.py       settings and their fallbacks
  parameters.py   reads deployment config from SSM at start-up
  cache.py        the in-memory index: what exists, what does not
  storage.py      S3 access and name-to-key rules
  logging_setup.py  JSON logging, levels, CloudWatch delivery
  views.py        HTML rendering; one definition of each error
  routers/        pages (home, errors), files (download), admin
  templates/      base, home, error
  s3_config.py    the S3 client
nginx/            config, shared proxy snippet, static fallback page
```

## How the cache works

A download never asks S3 whether the file exists — it checks a set in memory,
so the common case costs nothing. That set is kept current three ways:

1. **Rebuild** — one paginated `ListObjectsV2`, at start-up and on demand via
   the refresh endpoint. A single LIST returns up to 1000 keys, far cheaper
   than a HEAD per request.
2. **HEAD on a miss** — covers a file that appeared in the bucket since the
   last rebuild, so it is servable without waiting for anything. The result is
   remembered either way; a key S3 reports as absent is held in a *negative*
   cache for `negative_cache_seconds`, so a crawler hitting names that do not
   exist cannot turn into unbounded S3 calls.

The negative cache is why the refresh endpoint matters. If someone asks for a
file *before* it is put in the bucket, that miss is remembered briefly, and
adding the file out-of-band will not be noticed until the entry ages out.
Refreshing clears it:

```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost/api/refresh     # {"status":"ok","files":3}
```

Deletions work the same way in reverse: a key that disappears from the bucket
is evicted when a request for it fails, so the next one is answered from
memory.

## Endpoints

| Method | Path             | Purpose                                       |
| ------ | ---------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| GET    | `/`              | Front page                                    |
| GET    | `/docs/{name}`  | Download; streamed, never buffered whole      |
| POST   | `/api/refresh`   | Rebuild the index from the bucket             |
| GET    | `/api/logging`   | Current log level                             |
| POST   | `/api/logging`   | Re-read log level / debug flag and apply them |
| GET    | `/api/healthz`   | Liveness; does not touch S3                   |
| GET    | `/errors/{code}` | Error page, fetched by nginx (internal only)  |

Anything under `/api` answers with JSON. Everything else follows `Accept`, so
a browser gets the styled page and `curl` gets JSON from the same URL.

## Configuration

The bucket is a property of the deployment, not of the image, so it comes from
**SSM Parameter Store** at start-up. The container carries only `AWS_REGION`
and `SERDSAFE_PLATFORM_ENV` — both are needed to reach Parameter Store, so
neither can come from it.

`SERDSAFE_PLATFORM_ENV` names the environment (`dev`, `staging`, `prod`) and
everything else is read from `/<env>/...`, so one image runs everywhere. It is
required: a container that guessed its environment could serve another one's
bucket, so it exits instead.

Parameters are matched on the last segment of their name, directly under
`/<env>` — the read is **not** recursive, so unrelated parameters filed deeper
in an environment's subtree are neither fetched nor decrypted:

| Leaf name                 | Meaning                                    |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `s3_bucket`               | Bucket to serve files from                  |
| `s3_prefix`               | Key prefix within it                        |
| `negative_cache_seconds`  | How long a known-missing key is remembered  |
| `log_level`               | CRITICAL / ERROR / WARNING / INFO / DEBUG   |
| `debug`                   | `true` forces DEBUG, overriding `log_level` |
| `cloudwatch_logs_enabled` | Send log events to CloudWatch directly      |
| `cloudwatch_log_group`    | Overrides the derived group; rarely needed  |
| `cloudwatch_log_stream`   | Defaults to the container host name         |

Anything else under the path is ignored rather than applied, so an unrelated
parameter filed alongside cannot reconfigure the app.

Read **once, at start-up**, and deliberately not on the refresh endpoint: that
re-reads the bucket's *contents*, whereas re-reading *which bucket* while
running would let long-lived containers drift onto different config with no
deploy to explain it. If the path is unreadable or holds no recognised
parameters, the process **exits** rather than starting up healthy and serving
the wrong bucket.

## Logging

Every line is JSON, so CloudWatch Logs Insights can filter and aggregate on the
fields rather than matching substrings:

```json
{"time":"...","level":"INFO","logger":"app.main","message":"GET /docs/a.txt 200",
 "method":"GET","path":"/docs/a.txt","status":200,"duration_ms":15.8,
 "request_id":"b333621f114d6e66f27a6d46c413ab2f"}
```

nginx generates a request id, passes it as `X-Request-Id`, and logs it in its
own JSON access line, so one request can be followed across both logs:

```
fields @timestamp, level, message, duration_ms
| filter request_id = "b333621f114d6e66f27a6d46c413ab2f"
| sort @timestamp asc
```

Levels are used deliberately rather than everything landing on INFO: a request
is logged at the level its outcome deserves (5xx → ERROR, 4xx → WARNING, health
checks → DEBUG), and cache hits, misses and negative-cache hits are DEBUG.

**Health checks never reach CloudWatch.** A liveness probe runs every few
seconds for the life of the service; stored, that is a standing ingestion and
retention charge for the least interesting traffic there is, and it buries real
events. The filter is attached to the CloudWatch handler alone, so probes still
appear on stdout — useful when watching a container locally — and it keys off a
context variable rather than the path, so anything logged *while* serving a
probe is dropped too, not just the request line. This holds even with `debug`
on, which is when the volume would otherwise be worst. nginx does the same for
its own access log, by user agent (`ELB-HealthChecker`, `kube-probe`).

**Getting it into CloudWatch.** Everything goes to stdout, which is what a
container log driver collects — on ECS the `awslogs` driver forwards it with no
application involvement, and that is the recommended setup. For anywhere
without such a driver, `cloudwatch_logs_enabled` attaches a handler that puts
events into CloudWatch directly, batched on a background thread. Leave it off
where stdout is already being collected, or you pay to deliver every line
twice. If that handler cannot start — no permissions, unreachable endpoint —
it is logged and the app carries on with stdout; logging is never a reason to
be down.

Delivered directly, the container needs `logs:CreateLogGroup`,
`logs:CreateLogStream` and `logs:PutLogEvents` on the log group.

### Turning debugging on without a restart

`debug` and `log_level` are the one slice of configuration that can be re-read
while the process is serving. Debugging gets switched on precisely when
something is already wrong, and restarting a container to raise its verbosity
usually destroys the state worth looking at. Everything else — which bucket,
which prefix — stays start-up only.

```bash
aws ssm put-parameter --name /prod/debug --value true \
  --type String --overwrite
curl -X POST http://localhost/api/logging
# {"status":"ok","log_level":"DEBUG","debug":true,"reloaded":{"debug":"true",...}}
```

Set it back to `false` and call it again to return to `log_level`.

## Running it

```bash
SERDSAFE_PLATFORM_ENV=dev docker compose up --build
```

The backend reads Parameter Store and writes CloudWatch in whatever AWS account
its credentials belong to.

### Against a local emulator

`SERDSAFE_LOCAL_STACK` sends **every** AWS call — Parameter Store, S3 and
CloudWatch alike — to an emulator instead. Set it to the endpoint, or to `true`
for `http://localhost:4566`:

```bash
SERDSAFE_LOCAL_STACK=http://host.docker.internal:4566 \
  SERDSAFE_PLATFORM_ENV=prod docker compose up --build
```

Note the host name. The emulator listens on *your machine*, and `localhost`
inside a container means the container, so from compose it has to be reached at
`host.docker.internal`. Running the app outside a container, plain
`http://localhost:4566` is right.

Two things happen automatically when it is set: S3 switches to path-style
addressing (virtual-host style would look for `<bucket>.localhost`, which
resolves nowhere), and placeholder credentials are supplied if the environment
has none, since an emulator ignores them but botocore still insists on finding
some. The app also logs a warning at start-up, so a container pointed at an
emulator is never mistaken for one talking to AWS.

Whichever it is, the environment needs its parameters to exist:

```bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566 \
  ssm put-parameter --name /prod/s3_bucket --value file-repo --type String
```

Compose refuses to start without `SERDSAFE_PLATFORM_ENV`, and the container
exits if it is set to something that is not usable as a path segment. Locally
`~/.aws` is mounted read-only so the container uses the same credentials as
your CLI; `AWS_PROFILE` and the standard `AWS_*` variables are passed through
too. Deployed, neither is present and the task role is used instead.

Then http://localhost.

`s3_prefix` is optional and defaults to `uploads/`. A value without a trailing
slash gets one, and `/` means the bucket root — the only way to express that
through Parameter Store, which cannot hold an empty value.

### Getting files in

Files go into the bucket directly — the app has no upload endpoint and no
write permission:

```bash
aws s3 cp ./some-file.pdf s3://my-bucket/uploads/
```

It is then at `http://localhost/docs/some-file.pdf`, served straight away: an
index miss falls back to a single HEAD, so there is no need to wait for
anything. `POST /api/refresh` rebuilds the whole index when many files have
been added at once.

### IAM

The task role needs, at minimum:

| Action                      | Resource                                    |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| `ssm:GetParametersByPath`   | `arn:aws:ssm:<region>:<acct>:parameter/<env>` |
| `s3:ListBucket`             | the bucket, condition `s3:prefix` on the prefix |
| `s3:GetObject`              | `<bucket>/<prefix>*` — read only, no PutObject |
| `logs:CreateLogGroup`, `CreateLogStream`, `PutLogEvents` | the log group |

An SSE-KMS bucket also needs `kms:GenerateDataKey` and `kms:Decrypt`.

## Notes

- Requested names must be plain file names. Path-shaped input is rejected
  rather than flattened, so a caller never gets a file back under a name they
  did not ask for.
- nginx caps request bodies at 8k. Nothing here accepts a file, and the POST
  endpoints that exist carry no body.
- No infrastructure template is included here yet. Deployed, the container
  needs `ssm:GetParametersByPath` on its parameter path, plus `s3:ListBucket`
  on the bucket and `s3:GetObject` under the prefix — no write permission.
